    "description": "The Checkout API has an OrderController, OrderRepository, PaymentGateway, Clock, and Mailer. The controller creates orders and sends confirmation messages, while the repository and payment gateway differ between production, local development, and automated tests. Production uses PostgreSQL, a Stripe-backed gateway, UTC system time, and SMTP; tests need in-memory persistence, a deterministic clock, a fake gateway, and captured mail. The deployment process must choose these implementations once during startup, and route tests must be able to construct a controller with substitutes without invoking deployment configuration. The controller should expose only operations for creating and querying orders, and the payment, persistence, time, and notification collaborators must be visible in its public construction contract. A separate startup component is responsible for creating the implementation objects and connecting them to the controller, while the controller must not read environment variables or select concrete classes itself.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how the Checkout API receives its repository, payment gateway, clock, and mailer so implementations are selected once and remain replaceable in tests.",
    "notes": "dependency-injection fits the startup-selected collaborators visible in the controller's construction contract with test substitutes; separated-interface is the near miss for the contract-packaging axis, with registry supporting startup assembly. Checked against seed cc-module-wiring: same family and pattern but different system and emphasis, not a near-duplicate."
  },
